# Task: chebyshev_silu_d7
Degree-7 Chebyshev polynomial approximation of the silu activation on [-4, 4] (benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks).
Inputs: ciphertext `x` (values in [-4, 4]); plaintext scalars: `alpha` = 0.25 (maps x to z = x/4 in [-1, 1]), `one` = 1.0, and c0 ... c7, the Chebyshev coefficients (each one is the same in every slot).
Output: `y` = c0 + sum_k c_k T_k(z), with T0 = 1, T1 = z and T_k = 2 z T_{k-1} - T_{k-2}. The coefficients c1, c2, c4, c6 and c0 are non-negligible; the rest are ~1e-16 and can be dropped.
Any mathematically equivalent evaluation is allowed (another basis or evaluation order), as long as it only uses these inputs.

Correctness: every output must be within 1e-06 (absolute) of the expected values on all test cases (fixed, dense). Timing is measured on the dense case.

# Baseline kernel
```cpp
// Baseline generated by agent/tasks/gen_baselines.py; Chebyshev silu degree 7 on [-4, 4], as in benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext one("one");
  vector<Ciphertext> t(8);
  t[1] = z;
  Ciphertext z_square = z * z;
  t[2] = z_square + z_square - one;
  for (int k = 3; k <= 7; ++k)
  {
    Ciphertext z_t = z * t[k - 1];
    t[k] = z_t + z_t - t[k - 2];
  }
  Ciphertext result;
  bool has_result = false;
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Ciphertext term1 = t[1] * c1;
  result = has_result ? result + term1 : term1;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Ciphertext term2 = t[2] * c2;
  result = has_result ? result + term2 : term2;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Ciphertext term4 = t[4] * c4;
  result = has_result ? result + term4 : term4;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext term6 = t[6] * c6;
  result = has_result ? result + term6 : term6;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

# Baseline result
status: ok (correct on every case)
fhe() evaluation time (dense case, median): 307.11 ms
CKKS levels consumed: 7
CHEHAB depth (total, multiplicative): (19, 7); IR terms: 64
operations: ct_ct_mul=5, ct_pt_mul=5, relin=5, rotate=0, rescale=10, mod_switch=10, match_scale=7, add=14
max abs error vs expected: 1.14e-07

You have 8 attempts. Propose a faster kernel that computes the same outputs.

Idea: the result only needs even powers (c2, c4, c6, c0) plus the linear term c1·z. Write everything as a polynomial in z with T1, T2, T4, T6 expanded:

- T2 = 2z²−1
- T4 = 8z⁴−8z²+1
- T6 = 32z⁶−48z⁴+18z²−1

The even part is a polynomial in u = z²: E(u) = a0 + a1·u + a2·u² + a3·u³, so y = c1·z + E(u).

The coefficients a_i must be built from the plaintexts. Plaintext-plaintext arithmetic isn't allowed, so I fold them in via ciphertext operations, using the ciphertexts' own scaling instead.

Using x directly: z = alpha·x, so z² = (alpha·x)². Compute u = z·z (1 ct-ct mul after the z multiply), then u2 = u·u, and u3 = u2·u.

Coefficient terms: 
- a3 = 32c6, a2 = 8c4 − 48c6, a1 = 4c2 − 8c4 + 18c6 (T2 contributes 2c2·u, so a1 = 2c2 − 8c4 + 18c6), a0 = c0 − c2 + c4 − c6.

These need plaintext-plaintext arithmetic, which isn't allowed. Instead I keep the Chebyshev terms in separate ciphertexts and avoid building coefficients:

- A = c6·u (ct-pt)
- Use Horner on u with the T-polynomial coefficients held implicitly: P(u) = ((32c6·u + …)). I can't form the combined coefficients, so I use a structure of basis sums with doubling.

Trick: 2·t = t + t is allowed on ciphertexts. So build the basis polynomials as ciphertexts:
- T2 = 2u − one
- T4 = 2·T2² − one
- T6 = 2·T2·T4 − T2

That costs: z (pt mul), u (mul), T2² (mul), T2·T4 (mul), for a depth of about 4. This replaces the baseline's chain of z·t[k] products (5 ct-ct muls) with 3 ct-ct muls. The c-multiplies are 4 ct-pt, and c1·z can use z = x·alpha, so c1·alpha would be plaintext-plaintext; I use (x·alpha)·c1 instead.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = z * z;
  Ciphertext t2 = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext q = t2 * t2;
  Ciphertext t4 = q + q - one;
  Ciphertext p = t2 * t4;
  Ciphertext t6 = p + p - t2;
  Ciphertext result = z * c1 + t2 * c2 + t4 * c4 + t6 * c6 + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: with t = T2 = 2z²−1, the polynomial is y = c0 + c1·z + c2·t + c4(2t²−1) + c6(4t³−3t). Grouping gives y = (c0−c4) + c1·z + t·g, where g = c2 − 3c6 + 2c4·t + 4c6·t². Plaintext constants can be added to a ciphertext, so g is built from sums of ciphertexts and plaintexts without any plaintext-plaintext arithmetic. This keeps 3 ct-ct multiplications but needs only 3 ct-pt multiplications instead of 5, plus the one for z.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = z * z;
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext v = t * t;
  Ciphertext a = t * c4;
  Ciphertext b = v * c6;
  Ciphertext b2 = b + b;
  Ciphertext b4 = b2 + b2;
  Ciphertext g = b4 + a + a + c2 - c6 - c6 - c6;
  Ciphertext r = t * g + z * c1;
  Ciphertext result = r + c0 - c4;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: keep the same algebra but reorder the c6 product. Computing (c6·t)·t instead of c6·(t·t) takes the ct-ct multiply off the t² critical path, so it runs one level shallower and the following operations are cheaper.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = z * z;
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext w = t * c6;
  Ciphertext b = w * t;
  Ciphertext a = t * c4;
  Ciphertext b2 = b + b;
  Ciphertext b4 = b2 + b2;
  Ciphertext g = b4 + a + a + c2 - c6 - c6 - c6;
  Ciphertext r = t * g + z * c1;
  Ciphertext result = r + c0 - c4;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: use Horner form in t. Then g = t·(4·c6·t + 2·c4) + (c2 − 3c6), which needs only one ct-pt multiply (c6·t) instead of two, because the c4 constant is added as a plaintext rather than multiplied.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = z * z;
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext w = t * c6;
  Ciphertext w2 = w + w;
  Ciphertext w4 = w2 + w2;
  Ciphertext inner = w4 + c4 + c4;
  Ciphertext m = t * inner;
  Ciphertext g = m + c2 - c6 - c6 - c6;
  Ciphertext r = t * g + z * c1;
  Ciphertext result = r + c0 - c4;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: halve the Horner inner term once more. Computing t·(2·c6·t + c4) and then doubling the result saves a plaintext add (about 2.5 ms of encoding) and one ciphertext add.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = z * z;
  Ciphertext t = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext w = t * c6;
  Ciphertext w2 = w + w;
  Ciphertext inner = w2 + c4;
  Ciphertext mh = t * inner;
  Ciphertext m = mh + mh;
  Ciphertext g = m + c2 - c6 - c6 - c6;
  Ciphertext r = t * g + z * c1;
  Ciphertext result = r + c0 - c4;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 5 was within timing noise of attempt 4, so I'm returning to the attempt-4 structure. It uses 3 ct-ct multiplications, 3 ct-pt multiplications and depth 5. Further restructuring (for example a depth-4 Horner in u) would need many plaintext adds to build the combined coefficients, and each costs more than the level it would save.

